{"artifact_id":"Figure9","kind":"figure","asset_path":"figures/fig_2606.11173v1-Figure9-1-f27ed49b1df1.png","asset_sha256":"f27ed49b1df144a255aa28acfb8020215bc1c8e84a585324551a06aeb9f503f8","source_pdf_sha256":"9b5f2341cae526105df68ad8cf1f703c6cebd9c396e7b8abe838db7ca8dd1d14","revision_vid":"f858dc5bf23f1e1e2156bdf7db3a3f0e87606a5606dc414236598439f051beed","canonical_id":"2606.11173","section_vid":"6a20cc14ac4dc15bbf824c20329b00f210c2784f9b8b4ecf7d7bd98fc4504483","detector_fingerprint":"pdffigures2-0.0.12-SNAPSHOT","detector_metadata_sha256":"d705f789ca24e942d205d635577cee9b0807763b891edaebc0fc6313448eb49e","page_index":13,"region":{"x1":80.64,"y1":131.51999999999998,"x2":564.0,"y2":337.91999999999996},"caption":"Figure 9 Teacher prompt for RefSol. The reference solution provided in the dataset is the privileged context.","containing_section":{"relation":"containing","section_id":"sec-1~12","path_id":"1~12","title":"Reference solution: {reference_solution}","text":"Instructions:-Produce a fresh, self-contained solution to the original problem.-Use the reference solution only to ensure correctness; do not mention or refer to it.Let's think step by step and produce a final answer in the format \\boxed{}.Figure 9 Teacher prompt for RefSol. The reference solution provided in the dataset is the privileged context.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":22},"neighboring_sections":[{"relation":"previous","section_id":"sec-1~11","path_id":"1~11","title":"Teacher Prompt -RefSol (reference-solution conditioning)","text":"Question: {problem} A reference solution is given below. Use it only to ensure correctness.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":21},{"relation":"next","section_id":"sec-1~13","path_id":"1~13","title":"Teacher Prompt -StepAlignFB (step-aligned feedback conditioning)","text":"Question: {problem} Expert feedback on a prior attempt at this problem is given below. The feedback diagnoses where the attempt went wrong (by step number) and carries the corrected continuation.","truncated":false,"section_ordinal":23}],"reference_excerpts":[{"section_id":"sec-1~12","path_id":"1~12","title":"Reference solution: {reference_solution}","matched_form":"figure 9","excerpt":"Instructions:-Produce a fresh, self-contained solution to the original problem.-Use the reference solution only to ensure correctness; do not mention or refer to it.Let's think step by step and produce a final answer in the format \\boxed{}.Figure 9 Teacher prompt for RefSol. The reference solution provided in the dataset is the privileged context.","truncated":false}],"article_summary":{"source_id":"grobid:2606.11173#abstract","source_text_sha256":"56bb18e102c657ebb67a601c0ac3c2cca73a0bf942aab718c80c17ab9a90d4c9","text":"Conditioning a language model on additional context, such as feedback on a previous attempt, typically improves its response. Self-distillation trains the model to retain this improvement when the context is not present. The method works by matching the model's output distribution under two settings: a student that sees only the question, and a self-teacher that also sees the context. What the model learns therefore depends on what context the self-teacher receives, yet the design of this context remains largely unexplored.We study context design for self-distillation by training a solver on feedback from a frozen critic. We compare three conditions: (i) a binary reward (GRPO), (ii) the reference solution, and (iii) a step-by-step critique aligned to the solver's reasoning trace.Step-aligned critique yields the largest gains, outperforming GRPO by 16.11 points and referencesolution-conditioned self-distillation by 5.27 points (Avg@12). Per-token advantage analysis reveals why: step-aligned feedback targets only the tokens where reasoning fails, leaving correct behavior intact. Conditioning on the reference solution, by contrast, pressures the model to change its behavior at every token (even correct steps) because an alternative derivation inevitably differs in phrasing and approach. This suggests that structural alignment between feedback and the solver's reasoning is a key driver of self-distillation effectiveness.Prior work has used various forms of context for self-distillation: code execution traces (Hübotter et al., 2026), ground-truth solutions (Zhao et al., 2026), and feedback from other models (Song et al., 2026) or users (Wang et al., 2026;Buening et al., 2026). In all cases, the context is treated as a fixed choice. No prior work, to our knowledge, studies how the design of the context affects what the model learns.","truncated":false},"diagnostics":[],"total_context_chars":2951,"truncated":false}